# Fixed 2026-09-29, defect 131's class, lane X4. Symptom: in `}` followed by
# `x` on its line, closing an `if 1 == 1 {` body, the `x` went unsaid (lane
# X1's finding 4): the `{`'s own report, the habit's, made the `if` a
# statement that had failed, and the rest of a failed statement's line is
# dropped as its debris. Cause: the drop took the `}`'s line, which is not the
# line the statement began on. What follows a `}` that begins its line and
# closes braces opened above is now read as the end of the statement those
# braces close, told as any statement's end is: after an `if`'s body, after an
# `else`'s, after a head that failed, and after a map's `}` as before.

function main() {  #~ missing_body
    if 1 == 1 {  #~ missing_body
        print(1)
    } x  #~ expected_end_of_line
    if 2 == 2 {  #~ missing_body
        print(2)
    } else {  #~ missing_body
        print(3)
    } y  #~ expected_end_of_line
    if 3 == ) {  #~ expected_expression missing_body
        print(4)
    } z  #~ expected_end_of_line
    m = {
        "a": 1
    } w  #~ expected_end_of_line
    print(m)
}
